when the essay mentions the yellow wallpaper my mind went to papillon bc I hadn't read the yellow wallpaper yet and papillon was the best introspection of solitary confinement I could envision and about which I thougth many times. I'm fairly sure I never would have survived with that kind of nourishment.. but I think my mind would have stayed intact, fueled by spite, just like Charrière claims kept him going — pure defiance. 
And that's the thing about spite as fuel. People underestimate it. Mainstream wants to talk about positive motivation, finding your purpose, gratitude journals. But sometimes the thing that keeps you breathing is "don't let the bastards grind you donwn". Papillon's solitary confinement — if we take even a fraction of his account as true — was designed to break the body so the mind would follow. Starvation rations, darkness, silence. The theory being: eventually the animal need overwhelms everything else. But hate is caloric somehow. It keeps something burning when everything else has been taken. 
Lench et al. (2023), published through the APA — across seven studies, anger compared to a neutral condition resulted in behavior that facilitated greater goal attainment on tasks that involved challenges. Angry participants solved more puzzles, scored higher in video games, and were more persistent.
The neuroscience (I'll link the sources): anger is not a withdrawal emotion. It's an approach emotion. When people get angry, levels of the stress hormone cortisol actually drop, suggesting anger helps people calm down and get ready to address a problem rather than run from it. Left-hemisphere brain activation occurs on the same side associated with approach and positive affect. Anger and happiness are neurological cousins.
Jennifer Lerner at Harvard found that anger diminished the effects of cortisol on heart reactivity, meaning it literally buffered stress at the biological level.
compared to fear, which amplifies uncertainty and risk aversion, anger simplifies the emotional landscape, producing confidence and readiness for action. Depression is characterized by withdrawal, passivity, anhedonia — the inability to want things. Anger is the neurological opposite. It's approach. It's wanting turned up loud, and yeah sometiemes I'll dig inside me for a bit of anger, bc I feel depression rear it's ugly head. I wanted to go into this topic bc anger is everywhere: in the world of AM and in the mind of Ellis and in me. what fueled this video was anger. and it was fun. 



